# Coordinates of simple features: stat_sf_coordinates

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:giggly, path: Path.join(__DIR__, "..")},
  {:kino, "~> 0.19"}
  ])
```

## About this notebook

This notebook follows the examples on ggplot2's reference page for
[`stat_sf_coordinates()`](https://ggplot2.tidyverse.org/reference/stat_sf_coordinates.html), in
the order the page gives them. Each example shows ggplot2's code first and then Giggly's. An
example Giggly cannot draw yet keeps its ggplot2 code and names what it is waiting for, so the
page is also a list of what remains.

`stat_sf_coordinates()` reduces each geometry of an `sf` object, from R's `sf` package, to one
point, by default a point on its surface, and gives that point's coordinates as `x` and `y`. Text
and labels need exactly one position for each geometry, so `geom_sf_text()` and `geom_sf_label()`
place theirs with it. Giggly has none of it yet: it has no `sf` objects.

## A point in each county

The examples read the counties of North Carolina from a shapefile that comes with the `sf`
package, and run only where that package is installed.

```r
if (requireNamespace("sf", quietly = TRUE)) {
nc <- sf::st_read(system.file("shape/nc.shp", package="sf"))

ggplot(nc) +
  stat_sf_coordinates()
```

Not in Giggly yet: `sf` objects and `stat_sf_coordinates()`.

## Error bars at each county

```r
ggplot(nc) +
  geom_errorbarh(
    aes(geometry = geometry,
        xmin = after_stat(x) - 0.1,
        xmax = after_stat(x) + 0.1,
        y = after_stat(y),
        height = 0.04),
    stat = "sf_coordinates"
  )
}
```

The error bars take their positions from the coordinates the stat computes, through
`after_stat()`.

Not in Giggly yet: `sf` objects, `stat_sf_coordinates()` given to a geom as
`stat = "sf_coordinates"`, horizontal error bars, `geom_errorbarh()`, mapping `geometry`, and
expressions in a mapping, such as `after_stat(x) - 0.1` and the constant `0.04`.
